<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  QBX-230. A DESTROYED SLink IS PURGED FROM EVERY SELECTION ROOT, not just the
  active one.

  THE CRASH THIS GATES. Reported from a Windows run: SIGSEGV inside
  QObject::disconnect, reached from SApplication::unselectSLink
  (shell/src/sapplication.cpp) by a plain mouse press in the mixer. The frame
  that settles it is unselectSLink's own: the fault is at the
  QObject::disconnect call, which sits INSIDE `if( wasFound )`, and
  QList::removeOne compares pointer values without dereferencing. So removeOne
  had returned true: the selection list genuinely held that pointer, and the
  object behind it was already freed. The list had outlived the SLink.

  WHY THE PURGE MISSED IT, and why that was not bad luck. Selection is PER
  ROOT (proposal 09 section 3): one SSelectionList per arrangement, each
  persisting while its root is not the active one. But `addSelectedSLink`
  connects the link's destroyed() signal ONCE, with no record of which root it
  was selected under, and the handler removed it from `selectionList_` alone,
  which is the ACTIVE root's list. Destroying an arrangement root fires
  SViewTabs::onRootDestroyed (shell/src/sviewtabs.cpp), which calls removeTab,
  which makes QTabWidget emit currentChanged, which calls
  setActiveSelectionRoot. Qt emits destroyed() BEFORE deleting an object's
  children, so the root's own handler re-points the active root first and the
  children it owns then announce their deaths into the WRONG list:

      ~QObject(root)
        emit destroyed()  ->  onRootDestroyed  ->  removeTab
                                  ->  setActiveSelectionRoot( OTHER )
        delete children   ->  each SLink's destroyed()
                                  ->  removeOne on the wrong list, purging nothing

  So the stranding is BY CONSTRUCTION every time a root holding a selection is
  destroyed, which is why it reproduced on opening a project rather than
  appearing as a rare race.

  WHAT EACH SECTION PINS, AND THE COUNT ATTRIBUTE. `assert-selection` grew
  `root` and `count` for this case. `root` is needed because the claim is
  about a root that is NOT active, and the verb deliberately read the active
  list only. `count` is needed because the verb's other half converts the
  links to paths, which DEREFERENCES every one of them: on the stale entry
  this case is here to catch, that is undefined behaviour, so a path
  conversion could never be the oracle. A size is answerable from pointer
  values alone, so it still reports honestly on a list holding a freed
  pointer. That is the whole reason the gate counts rather than describes.

  FOUR DEFENCES, AND THE SABOTAGE TABLE THAT SAYS WHICH ARE PINNED. Each was
  reverted on its own, rebuilt, and run against this case plus select_all_scope,
  tabs_selection_scoped, deselect_all_scope, tabs_edit_in_arrangement and
  action_roundtrip:

    S1  purgeDestroyedLink purges only the ACTIVE root (the original defect)
          GATED
    S2  addSelectedSLink drops its "if not already there" guard
          GATED
    S3  the purge updates the lists but leaves each root's `current`
          GATED, but only because of the one-link block in section (a);
          it passed the whole case before that block existed, since with two
          links selected a root's `current` is null by construction
    S5  SApplication stops listening to SProject::arrangementUnregistered
          GATED by section (c)
    S4  setCurrentProject stops calling clearAllSelections()
          NOT GATED, and no .qxa can gate it: a load goes INTO the same
          SProject, so setCurrentProject is never called with a different
          pointer, and the real path (SMainWindow::closeProject, which deletes
          the project and builds a new one) has no verb. The defence stays in
          regardless: the sabotage shows only that no SCRIPT reaches it, not
          that anything else covers it.

  WHAT SECTION (c) ACTUALLY REPRODUCES, which is not what it was written for.
  It was meant to strand a FREED link through the reporter's save/load path. It
  does not, and the fix itself is the proof: a destroyed link is necessarily
  purged by purgeDestroyedLink, so an entry that SURVIVES a load belongs to a
  link that was never destroyed. clearArrangements() only removeRef()s, and an
  open arrangement tab holds a ref, so the old root and its clips stay alive
  while the name stops denoting them. Measured before the arrangementUnregistered
  listener existed: the "Drums" entry still held the previous document's link,
  resolving to the empty path "Drums:" (empty because the link is no longer
  reachable in the registered tree). Stale state, therefore, not a
  use-after-free. That is a second, lesser defect of the same family, and S5 is
  its fix.

-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="selection_purge_across_roots">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <!-- The master: one track, one clip. -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackIndex="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>

    <!-- Drums: one track, two clips. A different shape from the master's, so
         a path resolved against the wrong root cannot accidentally agree. -->
    <create-arrangement name="Drums"/>
    <add-track arrangement="Drums" index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackPath="Drums:0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>
    <add-sample trackPath="Drums:0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav"
                timePos="192000"/>

    <!-- ===== (a) A LINK DESTROYED WHILE ITS ROOT IS NOT ACTIVE ==========
         Select both of Drums' clips with Drums active, then make the MASTER
         active, so Drums' list is the inactive one, and destroy one of the
         selected clips. The purge must still find it. -->
    <open-arrangement-tab name="Drums"/>
    <activate-tab name="Drums"/>
    <select-all/>
    <assert-selection paths="Drums:0,0;Drums:0,1"/>
    <assert-selection root="Drums" count="2"/>

    <activate-tab name="Arrangement"/>
    <!-- Drums keeps its selection while inactive: that is the per-root design,
         and it is also exactly what makes the stranding possible. Asserted so
         a future change that simply dropped inactive selections could not
         pass this case by making the bug unreachable. -->
    <assert-selection root="Drums" count="2"/>
    <!-- The master root is EMPTY, and measured so: `add-sample` does not
         select the clip it adds. The element is the other half of the claim
         above – Drums holds two while the master, now active, holds none, so
         the two roots are genuinely separate lists and not one shared one. -->
    <assert-selection root="" count="0"/>

    <!-- `remove-sample` ends in `delete clipLink`
         (objects/cut/src/sremovesampleaction.cpp), so the SELECTED SLink is
         destroyed here, with the master active. On the pre-fix binary the
         handler removed it from the MASTER's list, found nothing, and left
         Drums holding a freed pointer. -->
    <remove-sample trackPath="Drums:0" clipIndex="1" timePos="192000"/>
    <assert-selection root="Drums" count="1"/>

    <remove-sample trackPath="Drums:0" clipIndex="0" timePos="0"/>
    <assert-selection root="Drums" count="0"/>

    <!-- ONE link selected, then destroyed. This shape is here because the two
         removals above cannot reach the `current` half of the purge: with TWO
         links selected a root's `current` is null by construction
         (addSelectedSLink nulls it as soon as a second link arrives), so a
         purge that updated the lists and left every `current` untouched would
         satisfy every assertion so far. Measured: that sabotage passed the
         whole case until this block existed. With exactly one link selected
         `current` IS that link, so destroying it must move both the list and
         `current`, and the pointer-value invariant that assert-selection
         checks for every root is what notices if it does not. -->
    <add-sample trackPath="Drums:0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>
    <activate-tab name="Drums"/>
    <select-all/>
    <assert-selection root="Drums" count="1"/>
    <remove-sample trackPath="Drums:0" clipIndex="0" timePos="0"/>
    <assert-selection root="Drums" count="0"/>
    <!-- Back to the master before (b): `set-selection` resolves its paths
         against the ACTIVE root, and (b)'s "0,0" means the master's clip. -->
    <activate-tab name="Arrangement"/>

    <!-- ===== (b) A DUPLICATE IS NOT APPENDED ===========================
         `addSelectedSLink`'s comment said "if not already there" from the
         first commit and the code did not check. `set-selection` is the
         unguarded caller: SApplication::setSelectionFromPathsFor loops over
         the paths and adds each, with no isSLinkSelected guard of the kind
         addSelectionFromPaths carries. Two identical paths therefore appended
         the same link twice AND connected it twice, and on destruction the
         first purge's disconnect dropped BOTH connections, because Qt skips a
         connection disconnected during emission. The second copy was then a
         dangling pointer nothing would ever purge.

         Note the separator: `set-selection` splits its paths on "|", while
         `assert-selection` splits on ";". Two verbs, two spellings. -->
    <set-selection paths="0,0|0,0"/>
    <assert-selection root="" count="1"/>
    <assert-selection paths="0,0" root=""/>

    <!-- ===== (c) THE REPORTER'S SCENARIO: OPENING A SAVED PROJECT =======
         A load runs SProject::clearArrangements, which unregisters every
         arrangement and drops the registry's reference: "may reach 0 ->
         deleteLater". So the old roots die on a LATER event loop turn, after
         the new document's arrangements have been registered under the same
         names, and that deferred destruction is what runs the cascade in this
         header. `wait-ms` is what lets the deleteLater queue run; without it
         the case would assert before anything had been destroyed and would
         pass on the pre-fix binary too.

         load-project REPLACES the project, so this is the LAST section and
         the final assertions below describe the FIXTURE, not what the
         sections above built. -->
    <open-arrangement-tab name="Drums"/>
    <activate-tab name="Drums"/>
    <add-sample trackPath="Drums:0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>
    <select-all/>
    <assert-selection root="Drums" count="1"/>

    <save-project
        path="../../build/test-output/selection_purge_across_roots/spar.qxp"/>
    <load-project
        path="../../build/test-output/selection_purge_across_roots/spar.qxp"/>
    <wait-ms ms="400"/>

    <!-- THE CLAIM: nothing the closed document held survives in any root's
         list. Drums is the root whose links were selected; the master is
         checked too, because the cascade moves the active root onto it and a
         fix that merely swapped which single root gets purged would leave one
         of these two non-empty. -->
    <assert-selection root="Drums" count="0"/>
    <!-- THE MASTER ROOT IS NOT ASSERTED HERE, and that is a gap rather than
         an oversight. Drums empties because `clearArrangements()` unregisters
         the name and SApplication now listens to arrangementUnregistered. No
         signal says the same about the MASTER root: it is never unregistered,
         and the document boundary that would clear it (setCurrentProject ->
         clearAllSelections) does not fire, because load-project loads INTO
         the same SProject. Measured after the load: the master entry still
         holds one link, resolving to the empty path "" – alive but orphaned,
         so stale state and not a use-after-free. Asserting count="1" would
         pin that staleness as intended, and asserting 0 fails, so the case
         asserts neither and the gap is filed instead. -->
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <!-- The FIXTURE's shape (see section (c)): the master's one track. -->
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
